# Trivy CVE exceptions for orkyo-community
#
# Format:
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN  # Impact | Reason | Added: YYYY-MM-DD | Review: YYYY-MM-DD
#
# Policy: time-bound only. Review date must be within 14 days of added date.
# Remove entries once the upstream fix is available.

# ── Trivy false positive ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Trivy reports fix as 12.6.5 but KC ships mssql-jdbc 13.2.1.jre11 (newer); false positive.
CVE-2025-59250  # HIGH | mssql-jdbc: improper input validation — Trivy compares the installed 13.2.1 against a fix list ending 13.2.1.jre11 and calls it older; the shipped JAR is the fixed version, false positive (re-verified 2026-09-12 on quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:26.7.3) | Added: 2026-09-12 | Review: 2026-09-26

# ── Keycloak base OS layer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2026-09-03: the KC-26.6.x-era entries that stood in this file (jackson-databind,
# micrometer, OpenJDK, libacl, pgjdbc, six netty, a jackson-core GHSA, and the two sqlite
# FTS5 ones) are gone from the image this repo actually ships — the nightly security refresh
# rebuilt the foundation KC base out from under them. Verified against the artifact CI scans,
# ghcr.io/kymr10n/orkyo-community-keycloak:sha-b1290a37, both with CI's flags and with
# --ignore-unfixed OFF: the only findings are the mssql-jdbc false positive above and the
# entry below.


# ── Keycloak embedded Bouncy Castle — CVE-2026-8763 / CVE-2026-13506 ────────
# 2026-09-19: bcprov-jdk18on 1.84 is bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 (the pinned base) and by
# 26.7.4, the newest published tag — both scanned that day with CI's flags, both report the
# same findings, so a digest bump cannot clear this. The fix is bcprov 1.85 and no published
# Keycloak carries it. CVE-2026-8763 is an X.509 Name Constraints bypass in certificate path
# validation; the realm configures no x509 authenticator, no truststore and no mTLS, so
# Keycloak never validates a client certificate against a name-constrained CA, and it sits
# behind nginx and Cloudflare. Mirrors the entries in orkyo-foundation (#197). Drop both
# when a Keycloak build with bcprov >= 1.85 is published; foundation #178 tracks it.
CVE-2026-8763  # CRITICAL | bcprov-jdk18on 1.84 bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 and 26.7.4 (fix 1.85) | no upstream Keycloak with the fix; X.509 name-constraint path validation is unused here (no x509 authenticator, no truststore, no mTLS); KC behind nginx + Cloudflare | Added: 2026-09-19 | Review: 2026-09-26
CVE-2026-13506  # HIGH | bcprov-jdk18on 1.84, same library and same fix version as CVE-2026-8763 | Added: 2026-09-19 | Review: 2026-09-26

# ── Keycloak embedded netty — CVE-2026-75595 (CRITICAL) ─────────────────────
# 2026-09-12: netty-handler 4.1.136.Final is bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 via the
# foundation image this repo layers on; the fix is 4.1.137/4.2.17 and no published
# Keycloak carries it. Same entry and reasoning as orkyo-foundation: production and
# staging ALREADY run this exact netty (deployed 0.22.0 image, digest dd1ae711…,
# scanned 2026-09-12), so shipping is not a regression, while holding the train keeps
# the base-image fixes and 0.23.0 out of production. Keycloak sits behind nginx and
# Cloudflare. Drop when a Keycloak build with netty ≥ 4.1.137 is published
# (foundation #176).
CVE-2026-75595  # CRITICAL | netty-handler 4.1.136.Final bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 (fix 4.1.137 / 4.2.17) | no upstream Keycloak with the fix yet; production already runs this exact netty (0.22.0 image, scanned 2026-09-12) so shipping is not a regression; KC behind nginx + Cloudflare | Added: 2026-09-12 | Review: 2026-09-26
